---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import lab from "../data/lab.json";
---
<Layout title="Methodology — Crawl Policy Index">
  <p class="kicker">Public contract</p>
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p>
    v1 measures <strong>robots.txt only</strong>. Crawl policy that lives in HTTP 402 responses
    or signed-agent headers is out of scope and must not be inferred from these numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>This page is lab output</h2>
  <p>
    Agents in <code>registry/agents.yml</code> currently ship with <code>verified: false</code>.
    The published resolution function <code>effective_policy(as_of_date)</code> therefore returns
    no rows. Figures on the lab tracker come from <code>{lab.view}</code> and must not be quoted
    as “the web.”
  </p>

  <h2>Panel</h2>
  <p>
    Every statistic is of a named, frozen panel version. The current lab panel is
    <code>{lab.panel_version}</code> (first 1,000 non-infrastructure pay-level domains from a
    pinned Tranco list). Re-running <code>panel/build_panel.py</code> with the same pinned sources
    and seed must produce a byte-identical CSV.
  </p>

  <h2>Detect gate</h2>
  <p>
    Bodies served at <code>/robots.txt</code> are classified before parsing. Rows classified
    <code>ambiguous</code>, <code>not_robots_html</code>, <code>not_robots_binary</code>, or
    <code>empty</code> are excluded from policy aggregates.
  </p>

  <h2>Effective policy</h2>
  <p>
    <code>(domain, agent) → state</code> is resolved only by <code>effective_policy(as_of_date)</code>:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>considers only <code>verified AND active</code> agents;</li>
    <li>uses an explicit <code>policy_interval</code> row when the file named the agent;</li>
    <li>otherwise uses the <code>*</code> group (<code>WILDCARD</code>) when one exists;</li>
    <li>otherwise reports <code>ALLOWED</code> / <code>NONE</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A domain with no trustworthy robots.txt is absent from the function, not counted as allowing crawlers.
    <code>EXPLICIT</code> and <code>WILDCARD</code> blocks are never conflated in a headline.
  </p>

  <h2>Tokens that are not crawlers</h2>
  <p>
    <code>Google-Extended</code>, <code>Applebot-Extended</code>, and other <code>opt_out_token</code>
    entries are control signals. They are never summed into crawler-blocking rates.
  </p>

  <h2>Reproducibility</h2>
  <p>
    Raw bytes are stored once per SHA-256 and never discarded. A parser bug is fixed by bumping
    <code>parse_version</code> and re-parsing blobs. Prior parse rows are kept.
  </p>
</Layout>
